        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Pavers Need Sealing -->
        <div id="why-seal-pavers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Pavers Need Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pavers — whether concrete, natural stone, or clay brick — are installed as individual units set into a compacted base with sand-filled joints between them. That joint system is both the paver installation's greatest strength (it flexes instead of cracking like a monolithic slab) and its biggest long-term vulnerability. <strong>Joint sand stabilization</strong> is the number one reason most homeowners search for a paver sealer in the first place: loose joint sand washes out gradually with every rainstorm and every pass of a leaf blower or garden hose, and once a joint empties out, the whole system loses interlock, allowing individual pavers to shift, rock, or settle unevenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Empty or loose joints also open the door to <strong>weed and ant infestation</strong>. Wind-blown seeds germinate easily in loose, aerated sand, and ants find the same loose sand ideal for tunneling and nest-building — both problems we see far less often on properly sealed, sand-locked joints. A quality sealer binds the top layer of sand into a semi-solid mass that still flexes with seasonal movement but no longer offers an easy foothold for weeds or insects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the joints, sealing dramatically affects the paver surface itself. Unsealed concrete and natural stone pavers absorb UV radiation unevenly, which over a few seasons leads to <strong>color fading and chalking</strong> — that dusty, washed-out look so common on older, never-sealed patios. A wet-look sealer restores and locks in vivid color; even a matte penetrating sealer with UV inhibitors slows fading substantially compared to leaving pavers bare. And in climates with real winters, <strong>freeze-thaw protection</strong> matters enormously: pavers absorb water into their surface pores, and when that water freezes it expands, gradually cracking and spalling the paver face over repeated cycles. A sealer — whether film-forming or penetrating — reduces the amount of water the paver absorbs in the first place, meaningfully slowing this freeze-thaw degradation.
          </p>

