        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Sealers -->
        <div id="types-of-sealers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Paver Sealers: Which Category Is Right for You?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest decision when shopping for paver sealer isn't which brand to buy — it's which <strong>category</strong> fits your goals. Every product in this guide falls into one of five overlapping categories, and understanding the trade-offs between them will save you from an expensive mistake far more reliably than comparing star ratings alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wet-Look Acrylic Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Foundation Armor AR350</strong> and <strong>EnduraSeal Wet Look Sealer</strong> form a glossy acrylic film on the paver surface that dramatically deepens and enriches color — the classic "always wet" look popular on patios, pool surrounds, and driveways. They also bind surface-level joint sand and add strong UV resistance. Best for: homeowners who want maximum color impact and don't mind a glossier, and therefore more slip-prone, surface (mitigated with a slip additive).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural-Look / Matte Penetrating Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Techniseal Perma Sealer</strong> and <strong>DryTreat Stain-Proof Original</strong> protect the paver and, in Techniseal's case, aggressively lock joint sand, while leaving the surface color essentially unchanged — no gloss, no darkening. Best for: homeowners and natural stone owners who want the paver to look freshly installed rather than glossy, or who specifically need joint sand stabilization without a shiny finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Rain Guard Blok-Guard</strong> and <strong>Super-Krete SK Guard</strong> use a water carrier instead of solvents, resulting in low VOCs, minimal odor, and easy soap-and-water cleanup. They're safer to apply around landscaping, pets, and pools. Best for: DIYers applying sealer themselves, environmentally conscious buyers, and anyone sealing near sensitive plantings or a pool that can't tolerate solvent runoff.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solvent-Based Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Most premium wet-look products, including <strong>Foundation Armor AR350</strong>, <strong>EnduraSeal</strong>, and <strong>Wet Look High Gloss Paver Sealer</strong>, use a solvent carrier that generally produces deeper gloss, stronger film build, and longer service life than water-based equivalents, at the cost of stronger odor during application and the need for good outdoor ventilation. Best for: contractors and homeowners prioritizing maximum durability and gloss over low-odor application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film-Forming Polyurethane / High-Build Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            A smaller category of ultra-high-build sealers uses polyurethane or hybrid polyurethane-acrylic resins to create a thicker, more abrasion- and chemical-resistant film than standard acrylics, at a higher price point. These are typically reserved for commercial hardscapes, heavy vehicular traffic areas, or homeowners who want the longest possible interval between resealing. Best for: driveways, commercial walkways, and anyone willing to pay a premium for maximum film durability.
          </p>

